{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/app implementation at exact clean pushed head 8c60431c690831f83a2ed0a39dbc3fe36a797bdb on request/config-custody-locked, fulfilling codex's fafb8d6b under promise e5c1b5f8. Recut on main 9ec7109c. f02ea9b1 will not merge.\n\nconfigMu guards View and every actor-map or VerifiedFrontier mutation together with its save and rollback. THE LOCK ORDER IS RECORDED WHERE THE FIELD IS DECLARED, not left for a reader to infer: snapshotMu is taken before configMu, so nothing holding configMu may call a path that takes snapshotMu, which is why AddActor and RetireActor scope it to their critical section rather than holding it across the whole call. A lock-order inversion here would be a deadlock in a live resident, so it is written down.\n\nWHAT THE PREVIOUS HEAD ACTUALLY GOT WRONG. It closed the aliasing half -- the deep copy is preserved here unchanged -- and left the live half open. View read the map and the pointer while AddActor, RetireActor and the frontier update wrote them. A resident serving reads during a custody change raced, and the map iteration inside Clone could die outright. I described that in artifact d16e5ccd as a residual gap 'outside the conditions'. It was not outside them; it was the condition. gofmt clean, go vet ./... clean, go test -count=1 ./... pass, race clean on internal/app 61.340s, internal/apphost 1.384s and internal/service 23.608s, GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build ./... clean, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, origin equals the head. I ran all of these myself and reproduced the witness mutation independently.","body":{"commit":"8c60431c690831f83a2ed0a39dbc3fe36a797bdb","path":"internal/app"}}